**Claim:** Yes, $|H| = |K|$.

**Proof.**

Let $[G:H] = p$ and $[G:K] = q$, where $p, q$ are primes. We show $p = q$.

**Case 1: $G$ is cyclic of prime order.** Then the only proper subgroup of $G$ is $\{e\}$, which has index $|G|$ (prime). Hence $H = K = \{e\}$ and $|H| = |K| = 1$.

**Case 2: $G$ is nonabelian simple** (so $|G| \geq 60$).

Consider the action of $G$ on the left cosets $G/H$. This yields a homomorphism
$$\varphi_H : G \to S_p.$$
Its kernel is $\operatorname{core}_G(H) = \bigcap_{g \in G} gHg^{-1}$, a normal subgroup of $G$ contained in $H$. Since $H$ is proper ($p \geq 2$), $\ker \varphi_H \neq G$; since $G$ is simple, $\ker \varphi_H = \{e\}$. Thus $\varphi_H$ is injective and $G \hookrightarrow S_p$.

Now compose with the sign homomorphism $\operatorname{sgn} : S_p \to \{\pm 1\}$:
$$G \xrightarrow{\varphi_H} S_p \xrightarrow{\operatorname{sgn}} \{\pm 1\}.$$
Since $G$ is simple and $|G| \geq 60 > 2$, this map cannot be injective, so it is trivial. Hence $\varphi_H(G) \leq A_p$, giving
$$G \hookrightarrow A_p, \qquad \text{so } |G| \mid \tfrac{p!}{2}.$$

By the identical argument applied to $K$,
$$|G| \mid \tfrac{q!}{2}.$$

Since $|G| = p\,|H| = q\,|K|$, we have $p \mid |G|$ and $q \mid |G|$.

Suppose for contradiction that $p \neq q$; without loss, $p < q$. Then $q \mid |G|$ and $|G| \mid \tfrac{p!}{2}$, so $q \mid \tfrac{p!}{2}$. But every prime divisor of $\tfrac{p!}{2}$ is at most $p$, contradicting $q > p$.

Therefore $p = q$, and
$$|H| = \frac{|G|}{p} = \frac{|G|}{q} = |K|. \qquad \blacksquare$$

